"""Reading aids for Paper VIII's original visual-guide drawings."""
DESCRIPTIONS={
 'three_views':('An array, storage counts and toy compilation costs','The panels show a dense array, storage counts for dense and factored forms, and toy compilation costs under different error budgets. The cost plot uses declared model values rather than measured timings.'),
 'work_storage':('Arithmetic work and stored data','The displayed counts compare declared representations of the same operator. They are model counts rather than measured device timings.'),
 'factored_application':('A block diagonal action plus a thin correction','The local block action and the low-rank correction contribute to the same output vector. The correction uses the complete input rather than independent packet-local inputs.'),
 'cancellation':('Support permits an interaction that cancels','Two contributions share an output address. Their signed values cancel, so structural support is an upper bound on the realized nonzero pattern.'),
 'block_error':('Blocks and coherent error contributions','Block addresses identify output rows and input columns. The displayed example retains the contributions needed to distinguish individual block sizes from the assembled operator error.'),
 'circulant':('A finite circulant and its coordinate description','The displayed finite arrays retain the cyclic boundary convention and exact coefficients. A structured representation changes the calculation without changing the requested map.'),
 'kronecker':('A tensor product acting on a vectorized matrix','The factor matrices determine the two-sided matrix action. The vectorization convention fixes the order and the transpose in the displayed identity.'),
 'toeplitz':('A Toeplitz action with explicit boundaries','The diagonals share coefficients, while the finite input and output boundaries remain explicit. The picture does not silently replace the map by periodic convolution.'),
 'walsh':('A Walsh transform factored into stages','Successive butterfly stages retain all intermediate coordinates. The diagram describes the supplied finite transform and its arithmetic structure.'),
 'bdlr_product':('Every term of a factored operator product','The blocks and thin factors show the local product and all correction terms. Keeping the factors avoids materializing a dense product but does not remove cross interactions.'),
 'compact_core':('A product compressed to its range coordinates','The outer range factors carry a smaller core into the ambient space. The displayed dimensions distinguish redundant inner coordinates from the actual range dimensions.'),
 'optimality':('A small-core approximation lifted isometrically','The core and ambient spaces are linked by the supplied isometric range factors. The comparison preserves the stated norms and rank constraints under those hypotheses.'),
 'error_chain':('Local perturbations propagated through a product','Each local error is surrounded by the factors on both sides. The displayed contributions show why a local error bound needs the later transport factors.'),
 'woodbury':('An inverse represented through a small correction','The supplied inverse conditions and thin factors identify the small system that remains. The diagram does not assert that every low-rank update is invertible.'),
 'triangular':('A triangular solve organized by dependency levels','The arrows identify predecessor data required before a level can be solved. Level order follows the declared dependencies rather than an arbitrary parallel schedule.'),
 'schur_fill':('Elimination creates a new interaction','The eliminated variables connect previously separate blocks through the Schur term. Signed contributions can cancel even when the structural fill pattern permits an entry.'),
 'krylov':('A Krylov orbit inside its packetwise extension','The two spaces use different permitted operations. Invariance under one operator does not give independent access to each packet component.'),
 'preconditioning':('Coupling measured after block normalization','The displayed normalization separates the block diagonal part from the residual coupling. The estimate is tied to the specified operator norm and hypotheses.'),
 'precision':('A continuous error budget and clipped choices','The plotted allocation follows the declared error model and cost data. Bounds on allowable choices clip the continuous solution and must remain part of the optimization.'),
 'discrete':('All options in a finite integer budget model','The entries retain the discrete option choices, costs and certified errors. Feasible combinations are compared under the supplied budget rather than by an unproved greedy rule.'),
 'tree_frontier':('Five legal prunings and their cost-error frontier','The finite candidate tree determines which subsets are legal prunings. The frontier compares all five choices and shows the declared budget optimum.'),
 'local_update':('Dependencies affected by a local change','The marked subtree identifies the states that depend on the changed data. Reuse of unaffected states requires the retained locality and margin hypotheses.'),
 'batch_shapes':('Shape batches and declared memory traffic','Matrices of compatible shapes can share launches. The diagram keeps launch counts and data movement as separate costs in the stated model.'),
 'amortization':('Setup cost and the first reuse win','The two affine cost models are compared at integer reuse counts. A tie is separate from the first strict win; these lines are modeled costs rather than timings.'),
 'contract_match':('Matching output contracts before comparing methods','Each route identifies the object it returns. A timing comparison requires the same requested output, validation target and measurement conditions.'),
 'end_to_end':('Local and thin contributions to one exact output','The displayed calculation follows the running input through the local product and low-rank correction. Their sum is compared with an independent dense reference.'),
 'complex_core':('Complex range coordinates and squared approximation error','The plotted errors correspond to retaining zero, one or two singular directions. The matrix labels distinguish Hermitian isometry from an incorrect transpose convention.'),
 'inverse_closure':('A closure-allowed inverse entry that vanishes','The graph has two signed routes to the final vertex. The dashed closure edge permits that address structurally, while the exact inverse entry is zero by cancellation.'),
 'shared_family':('One common pruning under changing reuse weights','Solid and dashed affine cost lines compare keeping and splitting the same supplied tree. Their crossing and the marked integer samples determine the declared shared optimum.'),
 'quantization':('Actual error sums and conservative ceiling envelopes','Solid segments show the actual sums and dashed segments their ceiling envelopes. A sound integer budget can reject a feasible real assignment; rounding down can instead give a false certificate.'),
}
FIGURES={name:dict(alt=alt,description=description) for name,(alt,description) in DESCRIPTIONS.items()}
